{"absent":["Full struct sk_buff, sk_buff_head, skb_shared_info, and skb_frag_t layouts beyond fields touched here.","Locking discipline and caller requirements for double-underscore unlocked queue and buffer helpers.","Failure modes and allocation policies of fragment and packet allocation helpers.","Semantics of peek helpers, flow dissection, checksum handling, and pull permission checks referenced but not routed.","Configuration conditions selecting the alternate tail-pointer representation.","Meaning and lifecycle of destructor, socket owner, unreadable, pfmemalloc, and zero-copy states beyond the touched transitions."],"dossier":"D17","findings":[{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_len","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_len_lockless"],"text":"Queue length observation is offered in plain and lockless forms, the latter intended for concurrent observation without holding the queue lock."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_head_init","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_head_init","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_head_init_class"],"text":"Queue head setup is offered as a lock-agnostic anchor reset plus fuller forms that also initialize the queue spin lock and optionally assign a lock validation class."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_insert","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_after","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_before","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_head","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_tail","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_splice","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_splice","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_splice_init","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_splice_tail","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_splice_tail_init"],"text":"Queue insertion covers single-element placement at head, at tail, after a predecessor, or before a successor, plus splicing a whole source queue into a destination at head or tail with optional reinitialization of the source."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_unlink","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_dequeue","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_dequeue_tail","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_purge_reason","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_purge","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_purge"],"text":"Queue removal covers unlinking a known member, dequeuing from head or tail with empty yielding absence, and draining a whole queue while attributing a drop reason to each freed element."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_is_nonlinear","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_pagelen","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_pagelen","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_frag_fill_netmem_desc","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_frag_fill_page_desc","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_fill_netmem_desc_noacc","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_fill_page_desc_noacc","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_len_add","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_fill_netmem_desc","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_fill_page_desc","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_fill_netmem_desc","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_fill_page_desc","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_fill_page_desc_noacc","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_add_rx_frag_netmem","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_add_rx_frag"],"text":"Length and fragment accounting covers nonlinear detection, paged length totals, fragment descriptor setup from page or abstract memory, receive-side fragment attachment, and joint adjustment of total length, nonlinear length, and true size."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_tail_pointer.alt_1","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_tail_pointer.alt_1","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_tail_pointer.alt_2","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_tail_pointer.alt_2","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_put_zero","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_put_data","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_put_u8","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_put_zero","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_put_data","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_put_u8","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_push","include/linux/skbuff.h::pskb_pull","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_tailroom","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_availroom","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reserve","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_tailroom_reserve","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_set_length","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_trim","include/linux/skbuff.h::__pskb_trim","include/linux/skbuff.h::pskb_trim","include/linux/skbuff.h::pskb_trim_unique","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_grow"],"text":"Linear area management covers tail pointer reset and offsetting, zero-fill and copy appends, headward growth, conditional headward consumption, headroom reservation, tailroom queries, reserved tailroom bounding by MTU, length setting, trimming, and growth with possible head expansion."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_inner_headers","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_mac_len","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_inner_transport_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_inner_transport_offset","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_inner_transport_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_inner_transport_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_inner_network_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_inner_network_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_inner_network_header_was_set","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_inner_mac_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_inner_mac_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_unset_transport_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_transport_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_transport_header_careful","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_transport_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_transport_header_careful","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_network_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_network_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_mac_offset","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_mac_header_len","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_unset_mac_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_mac_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_mac_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_pop_mac_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_probe_transport_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_mac_header_rebuild","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_mac_header_rebuild_full","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_inner_network_header_len","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_inner_network_offset","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_transport_offset","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_checksum_start","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_checksum_start_offset","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_inner_protocol","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_inner_ipproto"],"text":"Header location management covers outer and inner MAC, network, and transport offsets, including reset to current data, relative adjustment, unset marking, offset queries, MAC length derivation, inner header copying, tunnel protocol tagging, and conditional transport discovery."},{"kind":"entry_point","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::netdev_alloc_frag","include/linux/skbuff.h::netdev_alloc_frag_align","include/linux/skbuff.h::netdev_alloc_skb","include/linux/skbuff.h::__dev_alloc_skb"],"text":"Allocation entry helpers provide device-associated and device-agnostic packet buffer allocation and fragment memory allocation with optional alignment handling."},{"kind":"invariant","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_head_init","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_insert","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_unlink"],"text":"An empty queue is self-linked with zero count; insertion increments the count and removal decrements it, and unlinking clears the removed element links."},{"kind":"invariant","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_splice","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_splice_init","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_splice_tail","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_splice_tail_init","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_splice"],"text":"Splice operations are no-ops on empty sources; otherwise they join the source chain into the destination and add the source count to the destination, with init variants also resetting the source to empty."},{"kind":"invariant","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_is_nonlinear","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_tailroom","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_availroom","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_set_length"],"text":"Nonlinear status follows nonzero nonlinear data length; nonlinear buffers report no writable tail room or available room, and linear-only length setting refuses nonlinear buffers."},{"kind":"invariant","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_transport_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_transport_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_unset_transport_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_network_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_network_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_mac_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_mac_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_unset_mac_header","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_mac_offset","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_transport_offset","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_mac_header_len"],"text":"Stored header positions are offsets from the buffer start; resets anchor them at current data, set forms add a caller displacement, unset forms use an all-ones marker, and length or offset queries derive differences between stored positions or against current data."},{"kind":"lock","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_head_init","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_len_lockless","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_insert","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_unlink"],"text":"Full queue initialization owns setup of the queue lock, while length and linkage observation and mutation include forms that use single-copy access so selected readers can proceed without acquiring that lock."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_head_init","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_head_init","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_queue_len"],"text":"The queue head carries predecessor and successor anchors, an element count, and a spin lock; the list itself can serve as the link sentinel."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_len_add","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_tailroom","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_availroom","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_tail_pointer.alt_1","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_tail_pointer.alt_2","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_mac_len","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_inner_protocol","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_inner_ipproto","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_checksum_start","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_orphan","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_fill_netmem_desc"],"text":"The packet buffer exposes total length, nonlinear length, true size, head, data, tail and end markers, reserved tail room, MAC length, outer and inner MAC, network and transport positions, inner protocol state, checksum start, ownership and destructor state, and allocation flags."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_pagelen","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_frag_fill_netmem_desc","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_fill_netmem_desc","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_add_rx_frag_netmem"],"text":"Paged storage uses a shared info fragment count plus per-fragment memory handle, offset, and size fields populated through indexed fragment descriptors."},{"kind":"state_machine","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_insert","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_unlink","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_dequeue","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_dequeue_tail","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_queue_purge_reason"],"text":"Queue membership moves from absent to linked on insert and back to absent on unlink or dequeue; dequeue maps empty to absence and nonempty to removal plus return, while purge repeatedly dequeues until empty."},{"kind":"state_machine","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reserve","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_push","include/linux/skbuff.h::pskb_pull","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_set_length","include/linux/skbuff.h::__pskb_trim","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_grow"],"text":"The linear window evolves by reserving headroom, advancing the tail on append, retreating data on headward growth, advancing data on pull, and shortening or extending the tail on trim or grow, with total length tracking consumed or released bytes."},{"kind":"hazard","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_push","include/linux/skbuff.h::netdev_alloc_frag_align","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_mac_len","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_reset_transport_header_careful","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_transport_header_careful","include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_set_length","include/linux/skbuff.h::pskb_trim_unique","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_add_rx_frag_netmem","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_assert_len"],"text":"Several helpers warn or fail closed on misuse, including oversized push or receive size, non-power-of-two fragment alignment, missing MAC header when deriving MAC length, offset truncation, nonlinear use of linear-only paths, and trim errors treated as fatal."},{"kind":"hazard","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_orphan","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_orphan_frags_rx"],"text":"Ownership release invokes a destructor when present and clears ownership, otherwise requires no owner; receive fragments with zero-copy state must be converted to owned copies before receive processing."},{"kind":"hazard","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::__skb_fill_netmem_desc"],"text":"Filling a fragment from abstract memory marks the buffer unreadable for one memory class and otherwise propagates a persistent-memory allocation property from the backing page header."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::NET_IP_ALIGN","include/linux/skbuff.h::NET_SKB_PAD"],"text":"Alignment and padding facts: IP alignment value is 2 and default packet padding is the maximum of 32 and level-one cache line size."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/linux/skbuff.h::ENCAP_TYPE_ETHER","include/linux/skbuff.h::ENCAP_TYPE_IPPROTO","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_inner_protocol","include/linux/skbuff.h::skb_set_inner_ipproto"],"text":"Tunnel inner-protocol tagging uses value 0 for Ethernet protocol form and value 1 for IP protocol-number form."},{"kind":"stub","sids":["include/asm-generic/r